<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Sultanate of Mogadishu</h1><p class="st-meta">950 CE – 1600 CE (approx.) · capital Mogadishu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Muslim port city on the Benadir coast that grew rich on gold, textiles and Indian Ocean trade and minted its own coins.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Mogadishu grew in the 10th century as Arab and Persian merchants settled among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> communities on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benadir">Benadir coast</a>. By the 13th century it had become the leading town of the region, with stone mosques such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fakr-ad-din-mosque">Mosque of Fakr ad-Din</a>, built in 1269, and a ruling dynasty that took the title of sultan.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">gold trade</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a> for a time and exported its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. It issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins from the 13th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, who visited in 1331, described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-ibn-shaykh-umar">Abu Bakr ibn Shaykh Umar</a>, who spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> (Maqdishi) and Arabic and was advised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a> and a council of notables, and an elaborate court ceremonial.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 16th century Mogadishu fell under the influence of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ajuran-sultanate">Ajuran Sultanate</a>, and Portuguese raids disrupted its trade. With the decline of the Ajuran around 1700, power passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hiraab-imamate">Hiraab Imamate</a>. The old town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamar-weyne">Hamar Weyne</a> preserves its medieval core.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sultanate-of-mogadishu.jpg" alt="Sultanate of Mogadishu" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Replayerr · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mogadishu.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>950 CE – 1600 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mogadishu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abu Bakr ibn Shaykh Umar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Somali, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/somalia">Somalia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mogadishu Sultanate, Mogadishu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1275&amp;focus=sultanate-of-mogadishu" aria-label="Sultanate of Mogadishu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5939 2101 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sultanate of Mogadishu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6259 2357L6202 2325"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6202 2325h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6259 2357h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6259 2357h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mogadishu. <a href="/atlas?year=1275&amp;focus=sultanate-of-mogadishu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

